  "notes": "A long barn on its own axis north-south: thick walls of local field stone (grey, brown and rose cobbles - rubble) about 3.4 m high under a dark corrugated-metal pitched roof, its gable ends clad dark with the V-shaped glazed truss and a pale rendered panel below (the end elevation and the dusk views). From the aerial: the pitched hall down the middle (about 40 x 13 m, a 16 m roof including the strips), a flat-roofed strip of service rooms along the east side with the entrance in its middle, and a cane pergola on steel posts over stone footings along the west side, where the hall opens toward the Andes. This pass: the entrance is now what the front photo shows - a recess with two white corrugated sliding doors either side of an open passage that runs right through the hall to the pergola side (the view through the building to the mountains, the 'reveal' the article describes); the walls are rubble rather than dressed stone; the vines are bare winter vines as every exterior photo shows them - rows of grey stems a pace apart on pale gravelly ground, not green hedges (the plate is now gravel, the dusty ground of the aerial), with bands of dry grass between some rows; the poplar windbreaks are real poplars (tall narrow flames, bare and pale) in lines along the north and east edges instead of a few lollipops; the pergola's canes are cane-coloured. Guessed: which way is north (the aerial's orientation is unknown; the article's 'opens west to the Andes' was followed), the exact length and roof pitch from door heights, the basin's position east of the building, the placing of west openings, the vine rows' direction and spacing (thinned to keep the dot count), where the windbreaks run; the semi-underground barrel cellar to the south is not visible outside and is not modelled. Refined 24 Sep 2026: the water basin east of the building is gone - no photograph shows one and the article names none; vines fill its place. The vine stems lowered to 1.0-1.2 m, as the vineyard photographs show them (they stood 1.4-1.7 m and read as a field of posts). A lighter grey roof was tried (steel; an open mesh sheet over the dark) and read either silver or speckled on this steep pitch, so the corrugated roof stays dark. Refined 22 Oct 2026 (3 rounds, photos 00-17, the aerial 06 above all; the five biggest mismatches were the roof's colour, the vines up to the walls, the missing track and forecourt, the solid poplars and the entrance canopy): the roof is slate (a mid grey-blue, as in the long elevations 04 and 05) where it read near-black; the gable ends stay dark; the barn stands in a bare gravel yard: the aerial shows the vines starting 8-10 m off its walls, so the 136 stems within 4 m of it are gone (the rows begin where the clearing ends), a pale dirt track runs beside the pergola and a wide pale forecourt before the entrance, where the first pass planted stems to the walls; the 19 poplars are narrower, pale warm grey (stone) where they were fat yellow-tan flames: every exterior photograph shows them leafless and pale against the sky (open mesh crowns were tried and read as dark cypress columns); four dry shrubs 3-4 m across east of the building and a heap of white boulders before the door, as the aerial and photograph 04 show; a slatted canopy over the entrance strip (the aerial's grid of slats, about 12 m, between the strip's two white roofs). Measured from the aerial (10 px a metre): hall roof about 15 m wide, the entrance strip about 2.7 m and the pergola about 4 m, against 14.2, 4 and 5 here - left as built. Still guessed: the vine rows' direction, the roof's pitch, the cellar."
